cat > sections/intro.tex <<'EOF'
\section{Introduction}
Evaluating a language model means running it on dozens to hundreds of benchmarks, each costing compute, time and effort. Public leaderboards meanwhile hold a history matrix of scores of $M$ earlier models on $N$ benchmarks. An evaluator facing a new model can afford only $k\ll N$ runs and wants the model's scores on the other $N-k$ benchmarks. We ask three questions: \emph{which} $k$ benchmarks to run, \emph{how well} the rest can then be inferred, and \emph{how large} $k$ must be.

Existing work answers neighbouring questions. Efficient-evaluation methods reduce the number of \emph{items} inside one benchmark \citep{vivek2024anchor,perlitz2024efficient,polo2024tiny,kipnis2024metabench}; studies of cross-benchmark predictability show that leaderboard scores are low-dimensional and can be predicted from a few capability components \citep{ruan2024observational,polo2024sloth}; matrix completion assumes that the observed cells are random. None treats the choice of the $k$ benchmarks as a design problem and tests the answer where it matters: for a model family that is not in the history, and for newer and stronger models. Leave-one-model-out evaluation also leaves near-identical siblings of the test model in the history, which makes a prediction look easier than it will be.

We study the problem with a protocol that holds out whole model \emph{families} (and, separately, later release dates), fits everything on the training models before selecting benchmarks, and compares selectors and predictors on identical targets with family-clustered uncertainty (Section~\ref{sec:protocol}). The method is a known toolkit applied to a new matrix: a factor model with diagonal noise fitted to the history, Gaussian conditioning to predict the unrun benchmarks, and greedy Bayesian A-optimal selection (Section~\ref{sec:method}). We state a closed-form risk for it and check the formula in three tiers (Section~\ref{sec:theory}). We run everything on three complete leaderboard blocks: the English and the multilingual MTEB embedding results and the 57 subjects of HELM MMLU (Figure~\ref{fig:overview}).

