\section{Problem and protocol}\label{sec:protocol}
\paragraph{Setting.}
A history matrix $X\in\mathbb{R}^{M\times N}$ holds the scores of $M$ models on $N$ benchmarks with no missing cell (a dense pool; Section~\ref{sec:data}). A new model has an unobserved row $x\in\mathbb{R}^N$. The evaluator picks a set $S$ of $k$ benchmarks, runs the model on them, and predicts its scores on a set of target benchmarks $T$ disjoint from $S$. The selector and the predictor may use $X$ and the observed $x_S$, and nothing else about the new model.

\paragraph{Targets, splits and leakage.}
In the main design a target set $T$ of 30\% of the benchmarks is drawn at random, $S$ is chosen among the other 70\%, and the procedure is repeated over five random partitions per fold; every selector, predictor and $k$ is therefore scored on identical targets. (The evaluator's literal problem, $T=[N]\setminus S$, makes the targets depend on the selector; we run it as a secondary design for greedy and random selection, Appendix~\ref{app:protocol}.) The main split is a grouped 10-fold split that holds out whole model \emph{families}, so no sibling of a test model is in the history; the family is the organisation plus the first two name tokens, and organisation-level families are a robustness check. Secondary splits are a random 10-fold split, which shares families between history and test, and a time-forward split that trains on the models released before a cut (55, 70 and 85\% of the release order) and tests on the next block. In every fold, the standardisation (training mean and standard deviation per benchmark), the transform, the rank and the parameters of the factor model, the selection and the predictor's tuning use only the training rows. Scores are standardised with the training fold's statistics and all errors are on that standardised scale.

\paragraph{Metrics and references.}
The error of a model is the mean squared error over the target benchmarks; we report the root of its mean over test models (standardised RMSE) and use the squared version for closure. Three references bracket it. The \emph{column mean} predicts the training mean (floor); the \emph{leave-one-benchmark-out} (LOBO) reference predicts each target from all other $N-1$ benchmarks of the new model (the lower error of the factor-model and Ledoit--Wolf conditional predictors); the \emph{in-sample} fit sees the test model's targets and is drawn only as a labelled, optimistic bound. The \emph{closure}
\begin{equation}
C(k)=\frac{E_{\rm colmean}-E_k}{E_{\rm colmean}-E_{\rm LOBO}},\qquad k^*(c)=\min\{k: C(k)\ge c\},
\end{equation}
is the share of the gap between floor and LOBO closed with $k$ benchmarks, and $k^*(c)$ is the smallest $k$ on the grid ($k=1,\dots,10,12,15,20,30$, capped at half the benchmarks) that reaches level $c$; it is censored (``$>G$'') when the grid maximum $G$ does not. Closure is relative to LOBO, not to the noise floor. Uncertainty is a family-clustered bootstrap over families (2\,000 replicates; paired for differences between methods), and random selection is the mean of 200 draws, so intervals reflect family-level variation but not the choice of the pool.

\paragraph{Pre-specified criteria.}
Four hypotheses and their decision rules were fixed before the runs. H1: $k^*(0.9)$ lies within $[r_{\rm eff},2r_{\rm eff}]$ ``knee near $r_{\rm eff}$'' on most pools, where $r_{\rm eff}$ is the smallest rank within one standard error of the best held-out likelihood; the alternatives are ``dominated by a general factor'' ($k^*<r_{\rm eff}$) and ``above window'' ($k^*>2r_{\rm eff}$ or censored). H2: greedy A-optimal selection beats random selection, with a grid-averaged paired gap in RMSE over $k=1,\dots,\min(10,N/2)$ whose family-bootstrap interval excludes zero and which is at least 10\% of the random error. H3: random-split accuracy overstates accuracy for new families and new
