\section{Experiments}\label{sec:experiments}
All results are on standardised scores of models held out by family, with the factor model and greedy A-optimal selection unless stated, a fixed 30\% target design and family-clustered 95\% intervals (Section~\ref{sec:protocol}). Extended results are in Appendix~\ref{app:results}.

\subsection{Data and regimes}\label{sec:data}
We use three complete leaderboard blocks (Table~\ref{tab:datasets}). \textbf{MTEB-Eng} and \textbf{MTEB-Multi} are the models and tasks of the MTEB leaderboard \citep{muennighoff2022mteb} that form a dense block: English-only tasks with at least 120 models having all of them, and multilingual tasks with at least 100 models, reduced by greedy deletion to a block with no missing cell. They are separate pools and are not pooled with each other. \textbf{HELM MMLU} is the 79 models $\times$ 57 subjects of the MMLU scenario in HELM \citep{liang2022helm,hendrycks2020mmlu}, complete by construction. The MTEB models are embedding models, not generative language models, and the 57 MMLU subjects are subtasks of one benchmark, so neither pool is a sample of ``language-model benchmarks''. The dense pools are also a selected part of the leaderboard: the MTEB models outside the pool are larger and newer and score $0.7$--$0.9$ pool standard deviations higher (Table~\ref{tab:datasets}), so every result below concerns the pool. A first factor carries 56--80\% of the variance; the selected rank is $r_{\rm eff}=4,10,2$. The three pools have different noise regimes: MTEB scores have idiosyncratic benchmark noise, whereas HELM MMLU accuracies carry binomial sampling noise (about 10\% of column variance), so we do not average across them. Small leaderboards ($N<12$) are descriptive case studies in Appendix~\ref{app:small} and carry no $k^*$.

