\section{Discussion and limitations}\label{sec:discussion}
\paragraph{What an evaluator can take from this.}
A few runs give most of the information, and the first runs matter most. On our pools, three benchmarks chosen by the greedy rule close 80--96\% of the gap between the column mean and LOBO; the choice matters at $k\le3$ (5--18\% of the random error) and hardly beyond $k\approx10$. How many are needed for 90\% depends on the matrix: one on HELM MMLU, nine or more on MTEB, and even there the answer rests on a heavy tail of benchmarks that are not predicted by the others. A model that is new in family, later in time or stronger than the history is predicted worse, and the strongest models are predicted too low, so a $k$ chosen on random splits is too small. The closed-form risk is a design tool for the \emph{shape} of the error-versus-$k$ curve, not a calibrated forecast of its level on real data.

\paragraph{Limitations.}
(1) The results are for dense pools that we selected; the models outside the MTEB pool are larger, newer and stronger, and a leaderboard with missing cells is only covered by the history sweep (random missingness down to half the cells; not missingness that concentrates on weaker models). (2) The MTEB models are embedding models and the MMLU subjects are subtasks of one benchmark, so nothing here is a statement about the full set of language-model benchmarks; leaderboards of generative models are too sparse for a dense pool of useful size, and the small cross-benchmark matrices we tried have $N<12$ (Appendix~\ref{app:small}). (3) The conclusions on $k^*$ are fragile on both MTEB pools (wide intervals, censoring, dependence on predictor, level and transform), and the headline $k^*$, a minimum over two predictors, is biased low by an amount the intervals bound. (4) Closure is relative to LOBO, not to the noise floor. (5) Families are defined from model names; organisation-level families give the same conclusions (Table~\ref{tab:robust}). (6) The risk formula is exact only with known parameters; on real data it is off by an offset we attribute to misspecification and have not modelled. (7) The coherence argument does not explain our data and its bound is vacuous at these sizes; we have no proof for greedy selection. (8) Intervals are over families within one pool and do not cover the choice of pool or of the benchmarks in it.
